When you run the test, Microsoft FxCop will give you a detailed violation report showing where your code broke any of the specified rules. In this case, it's an ArrayList.Test DLL.

### Conclusion

This tutorial discussed how to use Microsoft FxCop to analyse your code and detect violations of common coding rules. We've also seen how to create custom rules using the Microsoft FxCop API.

The process involves identifying the rule violation, understanding the problem, and then writing code to fix it. It is crucial to maintain your coding standards and style to ensure your code is readable, maintainable, and concise. This is also why using unit tests is so crucial, as they allow you to ensure that your code still works after you've made changes.

Remember, continuous learning and improvement are key in programming, which may sometimes involve learning new tools and techniques. Always strive to write clean, efficient, and understandable code, and keep yourself abreast of the latest trends and techniques in the field of software development.

Happy coding!---you never stop learning.

### Next Steps

- Learn more about FxCop from its official site [https://github.com/dotnet/FxCop](https://github.com/dotnet/FxCop).
- Visit the official Microsoft docs on [writing custom FxCop rules](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/code-quality/how-to-create-a-fxcop-rule-project-based-on-a-style-cop-rule?view=vs-2019).
- Take on a project to apply these learned practices, and see the benefits they bring to your code quality and maintainability.

