using System.Collections;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestClass]
    [PexClass(typeof(ArrayList))]
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void RemoveAtOnIndexBetweenArrayBounds([PexAssumeValid] ArrayList arrList,
            [PexAssumeValid] int index,
            [PexAssumeValid] int value)
        {
            if (arrList.Count > index && index >= 0)
            {
                AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);
            }

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_IndexOfValue = arrList.IndexOf(value);
            bool Old_arrListContainsValue = arrList.Contains(value);

            arrList.RemoveAt(index);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_IndexOfValue = arrList.IndexOf(value);
            bool New_arrListContainsValue = arrList.Contains(value);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_Value", Old_IndexOfValue);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_Value", New_IndexOfValue);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_Value", Old_arrListContainsValue);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_Value", New_arrListContainsValue);

            // An assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that the Count is decreased after a 'RemoveAt'
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount - 1, New_arrListCount);

            // An assertion that the IndexOf value is greater than the removed index after a 'RemoveAt'
            Assert.IsTrue(New_IndexOfValue >= index);

            // An assertion that the Contains method returns False--------------------	        Assert.AreNotEqual(New_arrListCount, arrList.LastIndexOf(Old_x));
            
            //Assert that the count of the ArrayList decrements by 1 after being removed.
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListCount, Old_arrListCount - 1);
        }
    }
}

I want to change this test to allow for testing of ArrayList.Remove method with unit testing. How do I go about it?
I have tried to follow the steps given in this http://research.microsoft.com/collaborations/14/default.aspx which provides details about modifying P/Invoke stubs for unit testing.
I can't get around the error: "Could not resolve the type or namespace name 'System''" in using System; and 'Could not resolve the type or namespace name 'ArrayList''" and 'ArrayList could be namespace or type.'

A: Let's first start with the resolution of the error messages. You'll need to reference the correct namespaces in your tests:
using System;
using System.Collections;

For both of these issues, your project should reference mscorlib.dll, which should contain System and System.Collections namespaces.
Now, regarding your problem, in order to test the ArrayList.Remove method we might have to mock or stub the inner ArrayList object that the ArrayList class wraps around. In practice, unit tests should isolate the parts of the code that they are testing, so we don't want to test the ArrayList's implementation details. Instead we want to test that Remove method works as it's supposed to.
Instead of directly calling ArrayList.Remove method in our test we can:


*

*Use reflection to call the method, since it's internal.

*Create a wrapper interface that represents ArrayList and define only methods that we are interested in. In that case our test will depend on this interface rather than an actual data structure.

*Use a mocking framework like Moq to simulate behavior of ArrayList.


Option 2 is probably the most clean way to write the test. Your ArrayList interface could look something like this:
public interface IArrayList
{
    void Remove(int index);
    // add more methods that you want to test if necessary
}

Then in your test you can replace real ArrayList with such an interface:
public void MyTest(IArrayList list, int indexToRemove)
{
    // Arrange
    //...

    // Act
    list.Remove(indexToRemove);

    // Assert
    //...
}

Then you can write actual tests using Moq or NSubstitute framework for creating mock objects. Here is an example using Moq:
[Test]
public void MyTest_RemovesElementFromList()
{
    // Arrange
    var mock = new Mock<IArrayList>();
    mock.Setup(l => l.Remove(It.IsAny<int>())).Callback<int>(index =>
    {
        // assert that Remove was called with a valid index, etc.
        Assert.True(index >= 0 && index < someArrayList.Count);
    });
    var list = mock.Object;

    // Act
    list.Remove(someIndex);

    // Assert
    mock.Verify(l => l.Remove(It.IsAny<int>()), Times.Once);
}

This way you are testing only a method of an object and isolate it from other objects. You can test different scenarios and edge cases by modifying Setup logic in mock.
Also remember that in practice you have to test more than only removing method. You might want to add elements, retrieve elements, sort array list, clear it and so on.
In the end it's about writing isolated unit tests. Your tests should run quickly, be reliable and easy to maintain.

A: In addition to what @Vasily said, I should also add that ArrayList is not recommended for unit testing, because it has a public API that's not designed to be easily tested. 

The List<T> class provides these benefits:

*

*Supports generics, which allows for compile-time type checking when you use it.

*Is thread safe if it is used within a lock statement.

*Supports LINQ, which means you can use methods like Where() and Count(), and can iterate over collections in a LINQ way.

If you have time and effort, the ArrayList and other generic versions of collections (like List<T>) are much easier to test because they don't have protected methods that cause them to--------------------
            // The following code snippet can be used to prove that the operation was successful.
            if(Old_arrListContainsX) Assert.IsFalse(New_arrListContainsX); 

            // The following code snippet can be used to prove that count decreased the number of items in array by 1.
            if (Old_arrListCount > 0) Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount+1); 

            // The following code snippet checks that the index of removed element is -1 in new array.
            Assert.AreEqual(-1, New_arrListIndexOfX); 
        }
    }
}
```

